WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.140 --> 00:00:04.520
- [Bertie] Polar bears are
such icons of the Arctic.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:07.740
It's hard for anything else
to escape their shadow.

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:13.660
But what if I told you only
a few inches from the ground,

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:17.000
there's a host of less
celebrated little creatures

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:20.060
who've made a playground
of these brutal conditions

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:23.020
and defied all our expectations?

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:25.900
I'm not cute. I'm a killing machine.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.240
Not useless. Pillar of the ecosystem.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.340
Not adorable. Master of camouflage.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.980
Exactly. Hang on tight,

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:39.900
because these unsung heroes of the Arctic

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.440
are going to blow your mind.

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.000
Big time.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:58.700
At first glance, the Arctic
feels brutally devoid of life.

00:00:58.740 --> 00:01:00.220
Though a select group of animals

00:01:00.220 --> 00:01:02.380
have figured out a way to thrive.

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:08.480
But the real force here
works quietly underfoot.

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.620
Every winter, sea ice
starts small and delicate.

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.920
Then, it grows over
millions of square miles.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:18.080
How is it possible that this freezer

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:20.340
holds the key to our entire planet?

00:01:21.160 --> 00:01:23.320
Like a lung, sea ice breathes life

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.700
into the Arctic every year in a cycle

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:28.280
that impacts the climate across the globe.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.440
This is the story from the
edge of the Canadian Arctic

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.800
where it all starts.

00:01:35.980 --> 00:01:37.220
My name's Bertie Gregory

00:01:37.220 --> 00:01:39.860
and I'm a National Geographic
wildlife filmmaker.

00:01:41.060 --> 00:01:42.860
Everything is at stake.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:45.740
We're all waiting for the big freeze.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.020
It's the height of winter
on the edge of the Arctic.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:02.780
The landscape is now dominated by ice.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.280
For the next few months,
only the hardiest survive.

00:02:10.860 --> 00:02:13.120
Oh, my eyelids keep freezing shut.

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.180
You'd think that the
animals that were hardest

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.020
to film would be the polar bears.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:25.560
You know, the great wanderers.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.200
But really, it's these small animals

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:30.860
that prove me the most difficult.

00:02:33.160 --> 00:02:35.500
Come on. Where are you little guys?

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.900
You got to be so careful
not to breathe out

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:48.700
on your binoculars like I've just done,

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.840
because now they're frozen

00:02:50.840 --> 00:02:52.920
and I can't see anything through them.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:57.300
Oh, wow!

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.620
Though basically the
size of a domestic cat,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.740
don't let appearances fool you.

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:08.840
You know, you see this
arctic fox bouncing about.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.000
Little bundles of fluff with
their little smiley faces.

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.540
It's so easy to forget that they are

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.040
in fact little killing machines.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:22.580
Right now, they're looking for lemmings.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:25.400
A small little brown
rodent which are hidden

00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.820
in their underground lairs under the snow.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.840
And the fox is using its
incredible sense of hearing

00:03:30.840 --> 00:03:33.220
to basically listen for
the little footsteps

00:03:33.220 --> 00:03:35.800
of the lemming as it moves
about under the snow.

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.020
And when they've found a
lemming under the snow,

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:43.380
well they then have a pretty
extraordinary hunting strategy.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:02.580
I've totally found an arctic fox highway.

00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:03.880
You see in front us there's

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:06.380
practically more tracks
than there is snow.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.900
And that's because as long
as the weather's good,

00:04:08.920 --> 00:04:12.220
it'll just keep on hunting
and then place the leftovers

00:04:12.220 --> 00:04:14.280
in little underground meat lockers.

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.700
These foxes don't mess around.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.460
Some of them have been recorded

00:04:21.460 --> 00:04:24.940
making as many as 90
kills in a single day.

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:35.120
(snow crunching)

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:37.980
Ah, here we go. Right.

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:41.560
We know that somewhere on
this frozen little pond

00:04:41.560 --> 00:04:42.340
is an arctic hare,

00:04:42.340 --> 00:04:42.960
and the reason that I know that

00:04:42.960 --> 00:04:45.500
is because these are so fresh

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:48.020
you can see how crispy the edges are.

00:04:48.020 --> 00:04:49.880
With this wind, it would've blown out

00:04:49.880 --> 00:04:51.880
and be all rough by now.

00:04:52.240 --> 00:04:54.780
So, that's really cool.
We just got to find it.

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:56.640
They're the kings of camouflage.

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:06.340
I have walked past the spot
four times? Five times?

00:05:07.520 --> 00:05:09.320
And I can't believe I didn't notice it.

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:12.400
See right at the base of that big rock?

00:05:14.420 --> 00:05:17.980
These arctic hares are not stupid.

00:05:18.040 --> 00:05:21.300
Satten behind this rock
right out of the wind

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:23.820
and it's about minus 30 degrees Celsius.

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.720
So, I don't have full control of my nose,

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.760
lips, or eyebrows.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.900
And you'll also see that
every time it lifts its head

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.640
to check for predators,
it's not turning its head.

00:05:38.640 --> 00:05:39.860
It's not looking around.

00:05:39.860 --> 00:05:43.060
And that's because its
eyes can see together

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.680
360 degrees in all directions.

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:46.940
So, it doesn't need to look around.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:49.340
It can just head up, okay coast is clear,

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:51.760
and carry on eating.

00:05:57.520 --> 00:05:59.700
Man, they're quick!

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:01.860
They're one of the fastest
animals in the Arctic.

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.560
They can clock 37 miles
an hour at top speed.

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.140
With so little to hide behind,

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:11.540
who wouldn't want to
be fast in the Arctic?

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:14.600
Well, this guy.

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.180
The snowy owl gave up speed in favor

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:19.820
of a mightier secret weapon:

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:23.840
Silence.

00:06:25.660 --> 00:06:27.700
The velvety texture of its feathers

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.140
and their comb-like edges

00:06:29.140 --> 00:06:31.420
absorb all the sound they produce.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.640
Go ahead. Run.

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:38.100
You won't even know what got you.

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:42.200
No way!

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.120
Oh, it's eating it.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.400
Oh, no time for chewing.
Just swallowing it whole.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.820
Definitely not a good
day for this lemming.

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:55.860
Yet, many predators rely
on so heavily on them

00:06:55.940 --> 00:06:58.220
that a bad year in the lemming population

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:01.720
turns into a devastating ripple
effect across the Arctic.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:14.500
(wind whistling)

00:07:14.760 --> 00:07:16.680
Well, this blizzard's just picked up.

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:20.980
And up ahead, there's this
group of little brown birds

00:07:20.980 --> 00:07:24.000
and I want to know why anything
is flying around in this.

00:07:25.440 --> 00:07:27.760
The Arctic's brutal weather wards off

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.120
almost every bird on the planet,

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:33.240
except for a few determined
little characters.

00:07:33.240 --> 00:07:35.680
Snow buntings are an Ice Age bird

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:37.720
known for singing their little hearts out

00:07:37.720 --> 00:07:40.720
whenever the temperatures
start to fall well below zero.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:47.960
And then there's what
seems to be a snow chicken,

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:50.600
but these willow
ptarmigan are no amateurs.

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.560
In the autumn, they grow a solid clump

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:54.640
of rigid feathers over their toes

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.720
creating their very own little snow shoes.

00:07:57.720 --> 00:08:00.760
You see these little ptarmigan

00:08:00.760 --> 00:08:02.320
just scuttling back and forth.

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:03.280
I think I can see little berries

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.200
kind of buried under the snow?

00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.600
- Over there! Red fox! Red fox!

00:08:06.640 --> 00:08:07.400
- What?

00:08:07.400 --> 00:08:10.040
- See that? Grass mound.
It's behind the grass.

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:11.480
- Where? You sure it's a red?

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.480
- Yeah. Behind the grass,
the left-hand side.

00:08:13.920 --> 00:08:15.080
- Got it. Got it. Got it.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.200
In recent years, the red fox

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:19.080
has started invading the Arctic.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.200
- Man, they're so much bigger.

00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.000
- [Bertie] They don't just want

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:24.440
to claim their new territory...

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:26.280
- Arctic fox! Arctic fox!

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.400
- They want to hunt
down their competition.

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.680
That arctic fox is not
hanging around. It is gone.

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:55.640
I mean, that presence of a red fox

00:08:55.640 --> 00:08:59.400
is, wow, big problem for those arctics.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.320
The red's much bigger

00:09:00.320 --> 00:09:03.320
and it just totally out
competes the arctics,

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:08.920
and that's really a symbol
of the warming climate here.

00:09:09.080 --> 00:09:10.680
It's allowing the range of the red fox

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.200
to push further north into the Arctics.

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:21.120
Winter is tough as it
is. Food get scarcer.

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:24.240
Days get shorter and further north,

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:26.200
the sun disappears for months.

00:09:27.640 --> 00:09:29.960
But our unsung heroes are undaunted.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.440
They will dig, peck, fly, or
scavenge through the night.

00:09:35.920 --> 00:09:39.080
And for the sun, it's solar winds

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:41.080
magically find their way through

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:43.560
as millions of charges particles strike

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:45.360
the Earth's atmosphere.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:50.880
This place is out of this world.

00:09:51.980 --> 00:09:53.000
Woo-Hoo!

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.160
(gentle piano music)

